WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:01.240
(audience applauding)
- All right, everybody.

00:00:01.240 --> 00:00:03.720
We are back in it's American Heart Month.

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:06.790
Not long ago, my next guest
became an unlikely advocate

00:00:06.790 --> 00:00:09.670
for heart health, when his
own heart began to fail.

00:00:09.670 --> 00:00:11.760
You've seen him as
Lieutenant Randall Disher

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:12.877
on the long-running series,

00:00:12.877 --> 00:00:13.710
"Monk".

00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:14.543
He's joining us today,

00:00:14.543 --> 00:00:17.130
thanks to a successful heart transplant.

00:00:17.130 --> 00:00:19.870
Say hello to Jason Gray-Stanford.

00:00:19.870 --> 00:00:22.916
(audience applauding)

00:00:22.916 --> 00:00:26.249
(hip-hop music playing)

00:00:32.531 --> 00:00:33.364
I'm a hugger.

00:00:33.364 --> 00:00:34.197
- I heard you were a hugger.

00:00:34.197 --> 00:00:35.310
- I know this has been killing me

00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:36.372
'cause I have to like elbow people.

00:00:36.372 --> 00:00:37.205
- Yeah.

00:00:37.205 --> 00:00:38.038
- So--
- I know.

00:00:38.038 --> 00:00:38.871
- Normal and weird.

00:00:38.871 --> 00:00:39.704
- The crazy times we live in.

00:00:39.704 --> 00:00:41.270
- I know, I know, at least
we're in the same room.

00:00:41.270 --> 00:00:42.103
That's positive.

00:00:42.103 --> 00:00:44.040
So I'd ask how you're feeling

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:45.900
but, well, you posted this video,

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:47.250
so let's see it real quick.

00:00:50.815 --> 00:00:54.010
Like, I don't know if
anyone's ever done that.

00:00:54.010 --> 00:00:57.730
- It's me putting in the
work with the W-E-R-K.

00:00:57.730 --> 00:00:58.563
- Dude, I'm telling you.

00:00:58.563 --> 00:01:00.950
(audience applauding)

00:01:00.950 --> 00:01:05.490
First of all, you're making
those one things look easy.

00:01:05.490 --> 00:01:07.940
That's hell on earth, my
man, I've done that before.

00:01:07.940 --> 00:01:09.190
Those things are heavy.

00:01:09.190 --> 00:01:11.520
- Yeah, well, I tell
you, it took a long time

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:12.740
to get back to that point.

00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:14.140
- Yeah, I was gonna say, but you,

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:15.480
this is the interesting thing

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:17.270
that I love that people are gonna hear

00:01:17.270 --> 00:01:18.880
because you are a fitness guy.

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:21.130
Like you had, you were like the picture

00:01:21.130 --> 00:01:23.085
of health kind of walking around, right?

00:01:23.085 --> 00:01:23.918
And then--

00:01:23.918 --> 00:01:25.130
- Yeah, this is something
that really truly came

00:01:25.130 --> 00:01:26.081
outta left field.

00:01:26.081 --> 00:01:27.036
- Yeah.

00:01:27.036 --> 00:01:27.930
- And, you know, hit you on a Tuesday

00:01:27.930 --> 00:01:29.135
when you least expect it.

00:01:29.135 --> 00:01:31.353
And I was.

00:01:31.353 --> 00:01:34.520
About 2018, I was actually
working on a project,

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:38.559
and I actually, I was working
long hours as you do, right?

00:01:38.559 --> 00:01:39.392
- Yeah.

00:01:39.392 --> 00:01:41.270
- And when I was visiting friends,

00:01:41.270 --> 00:01:43.090
kind of burning the candle at both ends,

00:01:43.090 --> 00:01:45.900
and you know, sure enough,
I just felt a little off.

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:46.820
I thought it was the flu.

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:47.707
And I literally was like,

00:01:47.707 --> 00:01:50.090
"Aagh, get over this, get some rest.

00:01:50.090 --> 00:01:51.330
You'd be bounced right back".

00:01:51.330 --> 00:01:52.163
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:01:52.163 --> 00:01:55.428
- But something in the
back of my mind was like,

00:01:55.428 --> 00:01:59.420
"Give your doctor a call
'cause you're just about

00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:01.450
to fly back home to Los Angeles".

00:02:01.450 --> 00:02:02.850
And you know, I'm just not feeling right.

00:02:02.850 --> 00:02:04.120
So I do, and he says, "You know what?

00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:05.240
You should go get yourself,

00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:06.410
just go get yourself checked out.

00:02:06.410 --> 00:02:08.430
We wanna make sure you don't
have, you know, pneumonia

00:02:08.430 --> 00:02:09.573
bronchitis or something.

00:02:09.573 --> 00:02:10.406
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:02:10.406 --> 00:02:14.210
- You know, and so sure
enough I go to the doctors

00:02:14.210 --> 00:02:16.340
and they wind up doing an EKG.

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:18.875
And that doctor comes in and says,

00:02:18.875 --> 00:02:21.930
"Your heart's in a crazy arrhythmia.

00:02:21.930 --> 00:02:24.430
You need to go to the
emergency room, now".

00:02:24.430 --> 00:02:26.370
And the cardiologist comes in,

00:02:26.370 --> 00:02:30.280
and basically after doing a
barrage of tests, you know,

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:33.907
kind of talks to me and says,
"You've got heart failure".

00:02:34.965 --> 00:02:35.952
- And you're probably like,

00:02:35.952 --> 00:02:37.634
"This is unbelievable,
I take care of myself."

00:02:37.634 --> 00:02:38.710
- Once again.

00:02:38.710 --> 00:02:39.543
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:02:39.543 --> 00:02:42.140
- Once again, to me,
it's almost ridiculous.

00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:43.692
It's almost like I'm in denial.

00:02:43.692 --> 00:02:44.525
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:02:44.525 --> 00:02:46.640
- Like if you want the
truth, I'm like, come on.

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:47.473
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:02:47.473 --> 00:02:48.306
- I'm like, "No, no, I feel okay.

00:02:48.306 --> 00:02:49.310
Let's go, I'll get outta here

00:02:49.310 --> 00:02:51.920
and just get back to regular life".

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:56.920
But, you know, it was
stable for quite some time.

00:02:58.080 --> 00:02:58.913
- With like medicine?

00:02:58.913 --> 00:03:01.798
- With medication and
proper checkups, you know.

00:03:01.798 --> 00:03:06.450
Come to the end of 2019,
I'm in a spin class,

00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:09.156
and off I go, 20 minutes
later I wake up on the floor.

00:03:09.156 --> 00:03:10.890
- [Kelly] That's scary.

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:15.400
- With the spin class, all looking at me.

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:19.240
And they basically rush
me to the emergency room.

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:21.890
And really from that point on,

00:03:21.890 --> 00:03:26.890
it was kind of a rapid downhill descent.

00:03:27.150 --> 00:03:27.983
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:03:27.983 --> 00:03:29.569
- That doctor comes back in and says,

00:03:29.569 --> 00:03:31.393
"We have a donor heart for you.

00:03:32.550 --> 00:03:35.010
We have one that's available now.

00:03:35.010 --> 00:03:38.100
You have two hours to
decide if you want it".

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:39.510
So--

00:03:39.510 --> 00:03:40.960
- That's such a big decision.

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:42.460
- That lands, yeah.

00:03:42.460 --> 00:03:43.293
- Yeah.

00:03:43.293 --> 00:03:45.810
And you know, and as I say,

00:03:45.810 --> 00:03:46.643
you know, look at me,

00:03:46.643 --> 00:03:48.280
I'm lucky to be sitting here
talking to you right now

00:03:48.280 --> 00:03:49.113
because of it.

00:03:49.113 --> 00:03:50.198
You know, and because of the--

00:03:50.198 --> 00:03:52.260
- God bless the advances.

00:03:52.260 --> 00:03:53.580
- 100%, I mean.
- Yeah.

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:57.830
- And the expertise of my
wonderful, wonderful doctors

00:03:57.830 --> 00:03:59.780
and the nurses and all the staff.

00:03:59.780 --> 00:04:04.130
And most importantly, to my donor

00:04:04.130 --> 00:04:08.051
and the donor family who
without their selflessness

00:04:08.051 --> 00:04:11.060
and kindness and generosity,

00:04:11.060 --> 00:04:13.010
I mean, I ain't sitting here
talking to you right now,

00:04:13.010 --> 00:04:13.843
doing this.

00:04:13.843 --> 00:04:14.676
- Yeah.
- [Jason] Yeah.

00:04:14.676 --> 00:04:15.509
(audience applauding)

00:04:15.509 --> 00:04:16.555
- Now, we're gonna do this, yeah.

00:04:16.555 --> 00:04:17.388
- It's pretty crazy.

00:04:17.388 --> 00:04:19.400
- Well, Jason doesn't know this,

00:04:19.400 --> 00:04:20.690
but we have one of the doctors

00:04:20.690 --> 00:04:23.780
who played a big part in his
transplant journey on the line.

00:04:23.780 --> 00:04:26.560
So let's everybody say hello
to the medical director

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:29.119
of heart transplants at St.
Paul's Hospital, Vancouver.

00:04:29.119 --> 00:04:29.952
- When is he coming up?

00:04:29.952 --> 00:04:30.785
There he is.

00:04:30.785 --> 00:04:31.719
- Dr. Mustafa Toma.

00:04:31.719 --> 00:04:32.552
(audience applauding)

00:04:32.552 --> 00:04:33.850
- Dr. Toma.

00:04:33.850 --> 00:04:35.028
Hi, how are you, sir?

00:04:35.028 --> 00:04:36.720
- Jason, good to see you, man.

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:38.149
- Wonderful, wonderful to see you.

00:04:38.149 --> 00:04:39.910
What a surprise.

00:04:39.910 --> 00:04:40.743
They got me.

00:04:40.743 --> 00:04:42.516
I had no idea this was coming.

00:04:42.516 --> 00:04:47.516
- No, it's amazing what
you're capable of doing.

00:04:47.570 --> 00:04:48.500
Like we were saying, the advances.

00:04:48.500 --> 00:04:50.810
It's amazing that he's
able to live his life

00:04:50.810 --> 00:04:52.440
at the selflessness of this donor,

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:55.290
but at the same time it's kind of amazing

00:04:55.290 --> 00:04:58.460
that this person that was in
perfect health, basically,

00:04:58.460 --> 00:05:00.990
ends up having heart problems.

00:05:00.990 --> 00:05:03.881
Like, what do you think people
should take away from this?

00:05:03.881 --> 00:05:05.750
- Well, I mean, as you guys probably know

00:05:05.750 --> 00:05:08.590
heart disease is the number
one killer in north America.

00:05:08.590 --> 00:05:11.780
And it's really never too late for people

00:05:11.780 --> 00:05:13.060
or too early, to be honest,

00:05:13.060 --> 00:05:15.430
to get their blood, their heart checked.

00:05:15.430 --> 00:05:18.000
You know, they need to
start by exercising.

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:20.179
They need to watch sort of diet.

00:05:20.179 --> 00:05:23.200
They need to see their family
doctor on a regular basis

00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:25.560
and to make sure that their cholesterol

00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:28.250
and their blood pressure
are all controlled.

00:05:28.250 --> 00:05:30.330
And obviously they need
to listen to their body.

00:05:30.330 --> 00:05:31.250
Jason is living proof

00:05:31.250 --> 00:05:33.317
that heart disease can
affect young people as well.

00:05:33.317 --> 00:05:34.900
And I think he did a great job

00:05:34.900 --> 00:05:36.398
and really, he's really lucky

00:05:36.398 --> 00:05:38.350
that he's sought medical attention

00:05:38.350 --> 00:05:40.181
and look at where he is now.

00:05:40.181 --> 00:05:41.014
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:05:41.014 --> 00:05:42.553
(audience applauding)

00:05:42.553 --> 00:05:43.386
- Yeah.

00:05:43.386 --> 00:05:44.219
- I mean--.

00:05:44.219 --> 00:05:45.052
- Yeah, well it's bitsy.

00:05:45.052 --> 00:05:47.730
Hey, a lot of credit goes
to these guys, right there.

00:05:47.730 --> 00:05:48.563
- No, absolutely.

00:05:48.563 --> 00:05:49.967
But he is right.
- Dr. Toma and his team.

00:05:49.967 --> 00:05:51.100
- But he is right though.

00:05:51.100 --> 00:05:52.180
Also give yourself credit

00:05:52.180 --> 00:05:53.620
'cause where I'm from, it's like,

00:05:53.620 --> 00:05:55.440
unless you're like bleeding from your head

00:05:55.440 --> 00:05:58.240
and like, can't move,
like you just take it.

00:05:58.240 --> 00:05:59.210
Like where I'm from in the south,

00:05:59.210 --> 00:06:00.790
it's like there, you know,
you're taught as a kid,

00:06:00.790 --> 00:06:02.158
like, "You're fine, walk it off".

00:06:02.158 --> 00:06:05.300
So it's important to really
be in tune with your body.

00:06:05.300 --> 00:06:06.210
- It is absolutely.

00:06:06.210 --> 00:06:09.360
And then Dr. Toma knows,
I asked a lot of questions

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:11.670
and I always kind of wanted
to be a part of the process

00:06:11.670 --> 00:06:12.884
as best I could, you know, and--

00:06:12.884 --> 00:06:14.640
- Watch your life.

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:16.070
- Yeah, 100%.

00:06:16.070 --> 00:06:17.970
But I mean, as I say, you know,

00:06:17.970 --> 00:06:20.930
just the care and the tact and the grace

00:06:20.930 --> 00:06:23.870
and the expertise that I was provided,

00:06:23.870 --> 00:06:27.462
was so comforting when you go
through a big kind of moment

00:06:27.462 --> 00:06:29.060
like this.

00:06:29.060 --> 00:06:31.242
It's a big deal, man.
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:06:31.242 --> 00:06:34.010
- You know, and I thank you, sir.

00:06:34.010 --> 00:06:35.450
You know, and everybody, you know.

00:06:35.450 --> 00:06:37.283
You pass it on to everybody
else up there who knows.

00:06:37.283 --> 00:06:38.190
(audience applauding)

00:06:38.190 --> 00:06:41.740
- A quick reminder, February
is American Heart Month

00:06:41.740 --> 00:06:43.700
and a good time to
schedule a heart checkup.

00:06:43.700 --> 00:06:46.370
If you didn't just hear this
story and you didn't hear that,

00:06:46.370 --> 00:06:50.453
then listen, you just never
know and better safe than sorry.

